<h2 id="the-vocabulary">0. The vocabulary</h2>
<p>An <strong>experiment</strong> is anything with an uncertain outcome:
rolling a die, testing a patient, drawing a card.</p>
<p>The <strong>sample space</strong> <span
class="math inline"><em>S</em></span> is the set of all possible
outcomes. For one die roll, <span
class="math inline"><em>S</em> = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}</span>.</p>
<p>An <strong>event</strong> is a subset of the sample space — something
that either happens or doesn’t. “The roll is even” is the event <span
class="math inline"><em>A</em> = {2, 4, 6}</span>.</p>
<p>When every outcome is equally likely:</p>
<p><span class="math display">$$P(A) = \frac{\text{number of outcomes in
} A}{\text{number of outcomes in } S}$$</span></p>
<p>So <span class="math inline"><em>P</em>(even) = 3/6 = 0.5</span>.</p>
<p>Two facts we will lean on constantly:</p>
<ul>
<li><span class="math inline">0 ≤ <em>P</em>(<em>A</em>) ≤ 1</span>
always.</li>
<li><span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>Ā</em>) = 1 − <em>P</em>(<em>A</em>)</span>,
where <span class="math inline"><em>Ā</em></span> (“not <span
class="math inline"><em>A</em></span>”) is the
<strong>complement</strong> of <span
class="math inline"><em>A</em></span>.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Quick check.</strong> A single die is rolled. Write out the
event <span class="math inline"><em>B</em></span> = “the roll is greater
than 4” and compute <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>B</em>)</span> and <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>B̄</em>)</span>.</p>
<p><span class="math inline">$B = \{\rule{2.5cm}{0.15pt}\}$</span> <span
class="math inline">$P(B) = \rule{1.5cm}{0.15pt}$</span> <span
class="math inline">$P(\bar{B}) = \rule{1.5cm}{0.15pt}$</span></p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2 id="intersection-and">1. Intersection — “<strong>and</strong>”</h2>
<p><span class="math inline"><em>A</em> ∩ <em>B</em></span> is the event
that <strong>both</strong> <span class="math inline"><em>A</em></span>
and <span class="math inline"><em>B</em></span> happen. Read the symbol
as “and.”</p>
<p><strong>Example.</strong> Draw one card from a standard 52-card deck.
Let</p>
<ul>
<li><span class="math inline"><em>A</em></span> = the card is a heart
(<span class="math inline">13</span> cards)</li>
<li><span class="math inline"><em>B</em></span> = the card is a face
card — J, Q, K (<span class="math inline">12</span> cards)</li>
</ul>
<p><span class="math inline"><em>A</em> ∩ <em>B</em></span> = the card
is a <em>heart face card</em>. There are exactly 3 of those (J♥, Q♥,
K♥), so</p>
<p><span class="math display">$$P(A \cap B) = \frac{3}{52} \approx
0.058$$</span></p>
<p>Notice that <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>A</em> ∩ <em>B</em>)</span> is
smaller than both <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>A</em>) = 13/52</span> and <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>B</em>) = 12/52</span>. Adding a
requirement can only shrink an event — it never grows it. This is worth
saying out loud, because it is the sanity check that catches most
arithmetic errors later.</p>
<p>If <span class="math inline"><em>A</em> ∩ <em>B</em></span> is empty
— the two events cannot co-occur — we call them <strong>mutually
exclusive</strong> and <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>A</em> ∩ <em>B</em>) = 0</span>. “The
card is a heart” and “the card is a spade” are mutually exclusive.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Exercise 1.</strong> One card is drawn. Let <span
class="math inline"><em>A</em></span> = “the card is red” and <span
class="math inline"><em>B</em></span> = “the card is an ace.”</p>
<ol type="a">
<li>How many cards are in <span
class="math inline"><em>A</em> ∩ <em>B</em></span>? _______</li>
<li>$P(A B) = $ _______</li>
<li>Name two events in this deck that are mutually exclusive.</li>
</ol>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2 id="union-or">2. Union — “<strong>or</strong>”</h2>
<p><span class="math inline"><em>A</em> ∪ <em>B</em></span> is the event
that <span class="math inline"><em>A</em></span> happens, or <span
class="math inline"><em>B</em></span> happens, or both. Read the symbol
as “or.”</p>
<p>The tempting move is to write <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>A</em> ∪ <em>B</em>) = <em>P</em>(<em>A</em>) + <em>P</em>(<em>B</em>)</span>.
That is wrong whenever the events overlap, because everything in the
overlap gets counted twice — once in <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>A</em>)</span> and again in <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>B</em>)</span>. The fix is to
subtract the double-count:</p>
<p><span
class="math display"><em>P</em>(<em>A</em> ∪ <em>B</em>) = <em>P</em>(<em>A</em>) + <em>P</em>(<em>B</em>) − <em>P</em>(<em>A</em> ∩ <em>B</em>)</span></p>
<p><strong>Example.</strong> Same deck, <span
class="math inline"><em>A</em></span> = heart, <span
class="math inline"><em>B</em></span> = face card.</p>
<p><span class="math display">$$P(A \cup B) = \frac{13}{52} +
\frac{12}{52} - \frac{3}{52} = \frac{22}{52} \approx 0.423$$</span></p>
<p>Sanity check by counting directly: 13 hearts, plus the 9 face cards
that aren’t hearts, is 22 cards. ✓</p>
<p>When the events <em>are</em> mutually exclusive, <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>A</em> ∩ <em>B</em>) = 0</span> and
the formula collapses back to plain addition. That’s the special case,
not the general rule.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Exercise 2.</strong> In a class of 40 students, 22 have
written Python before, 15 have taken a statistics course, and 9 have
done both.</p>
<ol type="a">
<li>How many have done Python <em>or</em> statistics (or both)?
_______</li>
<li>$P( ) = $ _______</li>
<li>How many have done <em>neither</em>? _______</li>
<li>Sketch a Venn diagram and label all four regions with counts.</li>
</ol>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2 id="conditional-probability-given">3. Conditional probability —
“<strong>given</strong>”</h2>
<p><span class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>A</em> ∣ <em>B</em>)</span>
is the probability that <span class="math inline"><em>A</em></span>
happens <strong>given that we already know <span
class="math inline"><em>B</em></span> happened</strong>. Read the bar as
“given.”</p>
<p>Conditioning on <span class="math inline"><em>B</em></span> means
throwing away every outcome where <span
class="math inline"><em>B</em></span> didn’t happen. <span
class="math inline"><em>B</em></span> becomes the new sample space. So
we count the outcomes where both happened, and divide by the outcomes
where <span class="math inline"><em>B</em></span> happened:</p>
<p><span class="math display">$$P(A \mid B) = \frac{P(A \cap
B)}{P(B)}$$</span></p>
<p><strong>Example.</strong> Draw a card. What is <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(face card ∣ heart)</span>?</p>
<p><span class="math display">$$P(B \mid A) = \frac{P(A \cap B)}{P(A)} =
\frac{3/52}{13/52} = \frac{3}{13} \approx 0.231$$</span></p>
<p>Or just reason it out: we know it’s a heart, so there are 13
possibilities, and 3 of them are face cards. Same answer, and the
formula is only bookkeeping for that intuition.</p>
<h3 id="two-things-to-notice">Two things to notice</h3>
<p><strong>Order matters.</strong> <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>A</em> ∣ <em>B</em>)</span> and <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>B</em> ∣ <em>A</em>)</span> are
different questions with different denominators. Above, <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(face ∣ heart) = 3/13</span>, but <span
class="math inline">$P(\text{heart} \mid \text{face}) =
\frac{3/52}{12/52} = 3/12 = 1/4$</span>. Same numerator, different
denominator, different answer. <strong>Confusing these two is the single
most common mistake in all of probability</strong>, and the entire point
of Bayes’ Theorem is to convert one into the other correctly.</p>
<p><strong>Sometimes knowing <span class="math inline"><em>B</em></span>
tells you nothing.</strong> If <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>A</em> ∣ <em>B</em>) = <em>P</em>(<em>A</em>)</span>,
the events are <strong>independent</strong> and the definition
rearranges into <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>A</em> ∩ <em>B</em>) = <em>P</em>(<em>A</em>)<em>P</em>(<em>B</em>)</span>.
Two coin flips are independent. A card being a heart and being a face
card are also independent — check it: <span
class="math inline">3/13 = <em>P</em>(face ∣ heart)</span> and <span
class="math inline">12/52 = 3/13 = <em>P</em>(face)</span>. Equal, so
independent. But an infection and a test result had better <em>not</em>
be independent, or the test is useless.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Exercise 3.</strong> Roll two fair dice. Let <span
class="math inline"><em>A</em></span> = “the sum is 8” and <span
class="math inline"><em>B</em></span> = “the first die shows 5.”</p>
<ol type="a">
<li>$P(A) = $ _______ (there are 36 equally likely outcomes)</li>
<li>$P(A B) = $ _______</li>
<li>$P(A B) = $ _______</li>
<li>Is <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>A</em> ∣ <em>B</em>)</span> bigger or
smaller than <span class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>A</em>)</span>?
What does that tell you?</li>
<li>Compute <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>B</em> ∣ <em>A</em>)</span> and
confirm it differs from <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>A</em> ∣ <em>B</em>)</span>.</li>
</ol>
</blockquote>
