<h2 id="putting-it-together-the-two-way-table">4. Putting it together:
the two-way table</h2>
<p>Most real problems arrive as counts in a table, and every quantity
above can be read straight off it.</p>
<p>Out of <strong>1,000 people</strong>, 1% carry an infection. A test
is applied to all of them; among the infected it comes back positive 97%
of the time, and among the healthy it also comes back positive 3% of the
time.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Test <strong>+</strong></th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Test <strong>−</strong></th>
<th style="text-align: right;"><strong>Total</strong></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Infected</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: right;">9.7</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">0.3</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">10</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Not infected</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: right;">29.7</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">960.3</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">990</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Total</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: right;">39.4</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">960.6</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">1,000</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>(Decimal people are fine — these are expected counts, not a
headcount.)</p>
<p>Reading the table:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Intersection:</strong> <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>I</em> ∩ +) = 9.7/1000 = 0.0097</span>
— one cell, over the grand total.</li>
<li><strong>Marginal:</strong> <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(+) = 39.4/1000 = 0.0394</span> — a row or
column total, over the grand total.</li>
<li><strong>Conditional:</strong> <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(+ ∣ <em>I</em>) = 9.7/10 = 0.97</span> —
one cell, over its <em>row</em> total. Conditioning on <span
class="math inline"><em>I</em></span> means the “Infected” row is now
the whole world.</li>
<li><strong>The other conditional:</strong> <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>I</em> ∣ +) = 9.7/39.4 ≈ 0.246</span>
— the same cell, over its <em>column</em> total.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Exercise 4.</strong> Using the table above:</p>
<ol type="a">
<li>$P({I} +) = $ _______</li>
<li>$P(- {I}) = $ _______</li>
<li>$P(I +) = $ _______ <em>(use the union formula, then verify by
counting cells)</em></li>
<li>Are <span class="math inline"><em>I</em></span> and <span
class="math inline">+</span> independent? Justify with numbers.</li>
</ol>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Exercise 5 — the punchline.</strong> Compare your answers for
<span class="math inline"><em>P</em>(+ ∣ <em>I</em>) = 0.97</span> and
<span class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>I</em> ∣ +) ≈ 0.246</span>.</p>
<p>Both describe “the test and the infection agreeing.” In one sentence,
explain to a classmate why they are so far apart. Which of the two would
a patient actually want to know?</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2 id="answer-key">Answer Key</h2>
<p><strong>Quick check.</strong> <span
class="math inline"><em>B</em> = {5, 6}</span>, <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>B</em>) = 2/6 = 1/3</span>, <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>B̄</em>) = 2/3</span>.</p>
<p><strong>Exercise 1.</strong> (a) 2 (A♥, A♦). (b) <span
class="math inline">2/52 = 1/26 ≈ 0.038</span>. (c) e.g. “heart” and
“spade,” or “ace” and “king” — any two that can’t hold at once.</p>
<p><strong>Exercise 2.</strong> (a) <span
class="math inline">22 + 15 − 9 = 28</span>. (b) <span
class="math inline">28/40 = 0.70</span>. (c) <span
class="math inline">40 − 28 = 12</span>. (d) Python only 13, both 9,
Stats only 6, neither 12 — the four must sum to 40.</p>
<p><strong>Exercise 3.</strong> (a) <span
class="math inline">5/36</span> — the sum-8 outcomes are
(2,6),(3,5),(4,4),(5,3),(6,2). (b) <span class="math inline">1/36</span>
— only (5,3). (c) <span class="math inline">$\frac{1/36}{6/36} =
1/6$</span>. (d) <span class="math inline">1/6 &gt; 5/36</span>, so
learning the first die is a 5 makes a sum of 8 <em>more</em> likely; the
events are not independent. (e) <span class="math inline">$P(B \mid A) =
\frac{1/36}{5/36} = 1/5$</span>, which is not <span
class="math inline">1/6</span>.</p>
<p><strong>Exercise 4.</strong> (a) <span
class="math inline">29.7/1000 = 0.0297</span>. (b) <span
class="math inline">960.3/990 ≈ 0.970</span>. (c) <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>I</em>) + <em>P</em>(+) − <em>P</em>(<em>I</em> ∩ +) = 0.01 + 0.0394 − 0.0097 = 0.0397</span>;
by cells, <span
class="math inline">(9.7 + 0.3 + 29.7)/1000 = 0.0397</span>. ✓ (d)
No. <span class="math inline"><em>P</em>(+ ∣ <em>I</em>) = 0.97</span>
but <span class="math inline"><em>P</em>(+) = 0.0394</span> — wildly
different, so the test result depends heavily on infection status.
(Good! An independent test would be worthless.)</p>
<p><strong>Exercise 5.</strong> The infection is rare, so the 990
healthy people vastly outnumber the 10 infected ones — and 3% of a large
group (29.7 false positives) beats 97% of a tiny group (9.7 true
positives). A positive result is therefore most often a false alarm. The
patient wants <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(<em>I</em> ∣ +)</span>, but the test’s
advertised “97% accuracy” reports <span
class="math inline"><em>P</em>(+ ∣ <em>I</em>)</span>. <strong>Bayes’
Theorem is the tool that converts the number we are given into the
number we want.</strong></p>
